# Go API guidelines

Go Fiber API. Module path: `go-api`. Code and comments in **English**.

This file is the single source of truth for architecture conventions. Keep rules **generic**; use project-specific names only as **examples**.

## Layers (dependency rule)

```
cmd/*/di → interfaces/http → application/{command,query,event,registry} → domain
                ↑                              ↑
         presenter                   infrastructure (adapters)
```

- **domain** never imports `infrastructure`, `interfaces`, `application`, or `cmd`
- **application** depends only on `domain` (and small shared app helpers like `application/messaging` errors) — never concrete adapters
- Wire concrete implementations only in `cmd/*/di`
- HTTP handlers call Command/Query handlers **directly** — no generic `CommandBus`

## Package layout

```
internal/
  domain/<aggregate>/     entity.go, events.go, repository.go (Write/Read interfaces)
  domain/event/           DomainEvent interface
  domain/port/            Outbox, messaging, dedup, external ports
  application/command/    sync write use-cases
  application/query/      read models / views
  application/event/      async handlers (+ dedup wrappers)
  application/registry/   HandlerRegistry (Register / Dispatch)
  infrastructure/
    persistence/write|read|outbox|processed
    messaging/rabbitmq/   publisher, consumer, topology (retry/DLQ)
    config|<vendor adapters>/…
  interfaces/http/        handlers, middleware, dto, presenter
cmd/
  api/                    HTTP server
  worker/                 Outbox relay + main RabbitMQ consumer
  cli/                    SQL migrations (goose) + schema check
  <extra>/                Optional specialized binaries (scheduler, pipeline workers, …)
migrations/               *.sql (schema source of truth)
```

Examples of aggregates: `user`, `order`, `scan`, `media`, `subscription`.  
Examples of optional binaries: `scheduler` (cron), or dedicated consumers for heavy work (`metadata`, `heuristic`, `aimodel`).

## CQRS principles

- **Command ≠ async event.** Commands run synchronously in the HTTP request: persist aggregate, return ID (`201`). Publish events **after** commit via outbox (fire-and-forget).
- **Long / heavy work** uses async consumers or dedicated processes (`202 Accepted` + tracking when the HTTP client must wait on progress).
- **No physical read/write DB split** by default — same Postgres; write repos use aggregates, read repos return views (not domain mutation).
- **Version event types** from day one: `user.created.v1`, `order.paid.v1`, `scan.completed.v1`, …
- Prefer **one main orchestration worker** with internal dispatch via `HandlerRegistry`, not a giant switch.
- Prefer **one main queue per bounded context / product** initially (e.g. `domain.events` or `user.events`). Split queues when a stage is heavy, slow, or scaled independently.

## Domain events & aggregates

- Aggregates accumulate events in memory; expose `PullEvents()` / `recordEvent()`.
- `DomainEvent` must expose at least: `EventID()`, `EventType()`, `AggregateID()`, `OccurredAt()`.
- **`eventId` is generated when the event is recorded on the aggregate**, not at publish time.
- Domain entities stay free of DDL/`gorm` schema tags; persistence models live under `infrastructure/persistence`.

## Commands (write)

- Package: `internal/application/command/<aggregate>/`
- In **one DB transaction**: save aggregate + `outbox.StoreEvents(PullEvents())`.
- Outbox row `id` = domain `eventId`.
- Fiber handler invokes the command handler and returns immediately (no wait on RabbitMQ).

## Queries (read)

- Package: `internal/application/query/<aggregate>/`
- Return dedicated views (`UserView`, `OrderView`, …), not write-side aggregates when possible.
- Read repos may use optimized SQL against the same tables.

## Outbox

- Table `outbox_events`; store in the same TX as the write.
- Worker relay polls unpublished rows → publish envelope to RabbitMQ → set `published_at`.
- Envelope includes `eventId`, `type`, `aggregateId`, `occurredAt`, `payload`.

## Event handlers & registry

- Register orchestration handlers in `cmd/worker/di` on `HandlerRegistry`.
- If the project has specialized consumers, register **their** handlers in the matching `cmd/<binary>/di`.
- Multiple handlers per event type are allowed.
- Handler names for dedup are stable strings (e.g. `user_created`, `notify_user_on_created`, `publish_scan_created_realtime`).
- Prefer real side-effect handlers. Avoid empty log-only stubs unless useful for ops.

## Idempotence (`processed_events`)

- Dedup key: **`(event_id, handler_name)`**.
- For DB side effects: `MarkProcessed` in the **same transaction** as the side effect; if not inserted → no-op.
- For external side effects (email/API/storage): prefer provider idempotency keys; `processed_events` alone does not make external calls exactly-once.
- Use `application/event/dedup.With` (or equivalent) when wiring handlers.

## Retry / DLQ (RabbitMQ)

- Classify errors: `RetryableError` vs `NonRetryableError` (`application/messaging`).
- Topology: `queue` → `queue.retry` (TTL + DLX back to main) → after max attempts or non-retryable → `queue.dlq`.
- **Never** `Nack(requeue=true)`. Retries go through the TTL retry queue only.
- Invalid envelope / poison messages → DLQ (do not tight-loop).
- DLQ is for investigation/replay — do not auto-purge.
- Each consumer queue (main or specialized) owns its own retry/DLQ topology.

## Specialized workers (optional pattern)

Use dedicated binaries/queues when a handler is CPU/IO heavy, has different scaling needs, or a different failure profile.

Example pipeline shape (media / document processing):

```
resource.uploaded.v1
  → (worker) enqueue stage A
stage.a.v1 → (stage-a worker) → stage.a.done.v1
  → (worker) enqueue stage B
stage.b.v1 → (stage-b worker) → stage.b.done.v1
  → (worker) finalize aggregate
```

Keep orchestration (enqueue next step, finalize, cleanup, realtime) on the main worker when possible; keep heavy analysis on specialized consumers.

## Schema & migrations

- DDL lives in `migrations/*.sql` (goose). No GORM `AutoMigrate` for schema ownership.
- Persistence models must match DB columns; `migrate check` / boot-time `schema.AssertModelsMatchDB` **must fail** on drift.
- Add a SQL migration when changing tables; update persistence models accordingly.

## Naming

- Usecase/command/query/event packages by **domain** (`user`, `order`, `billing`), not vendor names.
- Vendor SDKs and HTTP clients live under `infrastructure/<vendor>/` (e.g. `clerk`, `stripe`, `minio`).
- JSON API/presenter tags: **camelCase**.
- Do not camelCase external contracts (webhooks, provider payloads, RabbitMQ envelopes already defined, etc.).
- DI folders: `cmd/<binary>/di` (manual composition).

## Where new code goes

| Kind | Place |
|------|--------|
| Aggregate / events / repo interfaces | `internal/domain/<aggregate>/` |
| Ports | `internal/domain/port/` |
| Command / query / event handlers | `internal/application/...` |
| Handler registry | `internal/application/registry/` |
| GORM write / read / outbox / processed | `internal/infrastructure/persistence/` |
| RabbitMQ | `internal/infrastructure/messaging/rabbitmq/` |
| External adapters | `internal/infrastructure/<adapter>/` |
| HTTP | `internal/interfaces/http/` |
| SQL migrations | `migrations/` |
| Composition root | `cmd/<binary>/di/` |

## Do not

- Import adapters from the application layer
- Introduce a generic `CommandBus` / `Dispatch(any)` unless already required
- Put DDL in domain entity tags
- Use `Nack(requeue=true)` for retries
- Split the main queue by every event type prematurely
- Put heavy CPU/IO work on the main worker when a dedicated consumer already exists for that stage
- Expand scope beyond the request (no drive-by refactors)
